WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.279
And now great moments and unintended

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.569
consequences.

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.879
Part one Siege Sails

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.799
The Year 1585.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.629
The problem the Spanish army is

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:10.919
besieging Antwerp.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.179
The shelling approaching merchant

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.069
ships and causing food prices inside

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.329
the city to rise.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.339
The solution enacts strict price

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.049
controls on food.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:20.669
Sounds like a great idea.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.379
With the best of intentions, what

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.269
could possibly go wrong?

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.799
It turns out merchants don't like

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.059
risking their lives, ships and

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.069
cargo, especially when their goods

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.929
fetch the same prices at ports

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.149
without incoming cannon fire.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.069
Artificially low prices also

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.379
fuel demand, causing food supplies

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.089
inside the city to plummet.

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.799
It wasn't long before Antwerp

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.389
surrendered, given the city

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.339
government blockaded itself

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.679
better than any army ever could.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.319
Part Two: Chimney Cheapskates.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.419
The year 1662.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.189
The problem, Charles the Second

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.539
needs more money.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:55.619
The solution?

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.089
A hearth tax.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:58.649
Since the number of fireplaces in a

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.299
building is considered a proxy for

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.339
wealth. This progressive property

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.679
tax scheme was sure to be a hit.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.629
Sounds like a great idea with

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:07.919
the best of intentions.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.169
What could possibly go wrong?

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.909
It turns out people don't like

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:13.079
paying taxes.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:14.249
They also don't like petty

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.049
constables and subcontractors

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:17.729
entering their homes to count

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.659
stoves.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.159
Many stuffed up their chimneys to

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.449
avoid the taxes.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.429
One intrepid baker even knocked

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.169
through the wall from her oven to

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.179
access her neighbors chimney,

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.039
causing a fire that destroyed 20

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.959
homes and killed four people.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:32.639
And since the revenue generated was

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.559
less than expected, it wasn't

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.419
long before the hearth tax also

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.889
went up in smoke.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.979
Part Three: Cash for Clunkers.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.809
The year 2009.

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.729
The problem a recession.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:46.769
And we need to save the environment

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:48.479
and domestic manufacturing.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.979
Plus, something about economic

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.079
inequality and, you know, maybe

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.819
just the reelection campaign, all

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:55.949
that all that was

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.939
the problem.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.899
The solution give away $1

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.809
billion in incentives to U.S.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.669
residents who destroy their old

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:04.559
vehicles for more fuel efficient

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:05.879
new ones.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.709
Sounds like a great idea with

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.418
the best of intentions, what could

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.159
possibly go wrong?

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:13.439
It turns out people like free money.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:14.699
The program blew through the

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:16.469
original allocation in less than a

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.299
month, so Congress approved an

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.309
additional $2 billion,

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.049
which was gone a month later.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.089
Another thing people like driving

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:25.379
their new cars.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:26.819
While the program did increase

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:28.469
average fuel economy in the United

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.869
States by .65

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.189
miles per gallon.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.079
Total fuel consumption actually

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.359
rose more than 800 million

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.979
gallons in 2010.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.839
As for the economy, the boost in

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.789
vehicle sales was fully offset

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:43.469
by a fall off once the program

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.419
ended. In fact, for every job

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.129
created, the government spent $1.4

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:50.129
million on the program.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.419
But did it help U.S.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.249
car manufacturers?

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.139
Not really. Only two of the top ten

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.999
models sold as part of the program

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.859
were domestic brands and

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.629
apparently destroying an entire

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.499
generation of used cars causes

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.469
remaining used car prices to

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:05.249
rise.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.079
But hey, those are just the kinds

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.999
of vehicles less affluent people

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.859
buy, the kind donated

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:12.719
to charities or sold to

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.489
poor countries where they replace

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.559
even older, less fuel efficient

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:18.449
vehicles. And about that reelection

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:19.450
campaign?

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.070
Well,

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:24.329
I think it's important that we

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.249
consider great moments in unintended

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.499
consequences, good intentions,

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.149
bad results.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.469
Do you have an idea for a future

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:32.969
episode? Put it in the comments

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.799
below. We might use them or

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:35.879
not. Who knows?

